    'The vulva drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, then the deep inguinofemoral nodes (including Cloquet node) and on to the external iliac nodes; midline structures (clitoris, perineum) drain bilaterally. Vulval squamous cell carcinoma (in older women, often with lichen sclerosus or differentiated VIN; in younger women, HPV-related) spreads mainly by lymphatics. Tumours invading more than 1 mm need groin node assessment: sentinel lymph node biopsy for unifocal tumours under 4 cm with clinically negative nodes, otherwise inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy (ipsilateral for lateral tumours, bilateral for midline). Wide local excision with clear margins treats the primary.',
