    'In a pregnancy of unknown location, serial β-hCG measurements 48 hours apart help classify risk. In a viable intrauterine pregnancy, hCG usually rises by at least 35–50% over 48 hours in early pregnancy (classically about 66% or doubling). A plateau or a suboptimal rise suggests an ectopic or failing intrauterine pregnancy, and a fall of more than 50% suggests a failing pregnancy (e.g., complete miscarriage). Further serial hCG and repeat ultrasound are needed, as the woman remains at risk of ectopic pregnancy until resolved; very high or rapidly rising hCG suggests molar or multiple pregnancy.',
