    'Precocious puberty is the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before 8 years in girls and 9 years in boys. Central precocious puberty results from early activation of the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis: pubertal LH response to GnRH (or raised basal LH), advanced bone age and accelerated growth. It is usually idiopathic in girls, but MRI is done to exclude CNS lesions (hypothalamic hamartoma, tumours), which are more common in boys. Treatment is a long-acting GnRH agonist (e.g., leuprolide), which desensitises the pituitary, halts progression and preserves adult height. Peripheral causes include ovarian, adrenal and McCune–Albright syndrome.',
